# The Pharaoh's Game: Agent Rules & Coding Guidelines

## Python Configuration
- Always run python scripts using `python` instead of `python3` command.

## Version Control & Workflow
- Commit and push changes to GitHub after every successful modification to the project.
- Check the git diff before discarding/reverting any changes to avoid overwriting the user's modifications.
- Do not make redundant changes; verify the state of the repository before writing code.

## Optimization & Mobile Performance
- Aim for 30-60 FPS on mobile target devices.
- Use dynamic distance-based culling (`DistanceCuller.cs`) for renderers and colliders that are far away (>180 units) from the player.
- Minimize real-time light sources; prefer baked lighting or emissive materials for window/plaza lights.
- Batch static scenery objects using static batching.

## UI Design & Aesthetics
- Use premium color palettes: obsidian, clean charcoal, and Egyptian gold trim.
- Buttons should have high contrast (e.g., gold-yellow background with bold black text).
- Use normal-mapped UI frames (`UINormalMappedFrame.shader`) for high-quality embossed lighting effects.
- Canvas matching should prevent oval joysticks and scaling skew on wide mobile screens.

## Navigation & AI
- Set `NavMeshObstacles` with `carve = true` on all generated physical barriers (houses, columns, obelisks, tombs) to prevent AI stuck states.
- Set appropriate agent radii (e.g., 0.8f for standard mummies, 1.5f for Pharaoh bosses) to match their physical size.
- Maintain a strict limit of 20 active mummies to preserve CPU performance on mobile.
